{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_198","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' frontier_brief.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Unverified frontier brief: dc22 level 6\n\nThis is a compact index of the latest clean WIP, not solver evidence.\nReproduce every observation you rely on with the documented local API.\nDo not reread the full proposer transcript unless a named ambiguity requires it.\n\n## Verified parent budget\n\n- Exact parent boundary: level 5 at 305 actions.\n- Remaining real-action budget under the harness cap: 295 of 600.\n- This budget is verifier evidence. If it is insufficient, optimize earlier composed legs rather than searching an uncommittable suffix.\n\n## Prior proposer progress\n\n- The centered geometry suggests a transfer mechanic rather than a terminal: enclose the cyan/brown inner piece, toggle the rotator to attach it, then move the outer ring while carrying that piece. I’m checking the main-aware ring state graph and the inner piece’s bbox after leaving the dock; this is the exact level-5-style operation that plain placement enumeration would miss.\n- Docking and departing after the rotator toggle is frame-identical to an ordinary ring move, so there is no attachment. One interaction class remains untested: entering a ring endpoint while the global bridge, rotator, or selector is already in a particular phase. I’m exhaustively checking every ring transition under all 48 global phase combinations; this is finite and directly tests synchronization.\n- All synchronized ring transitions fail, so the ring has no reward-bearing endpoint under any global phase. I’m now running a tighter macro planner over the verified action model. It normalizes ordinary avatar positions into movement components while preserving world pixels, selector phase, control effects, and the exact action path; this avoids the position explosion that stopped the earlier full-state search.\n- The normalized planner is now reaching canonical states, but it was still queueing the same remote component once per avatar cell. I stopped it after confirming that pattern. I’m tightening the queue key to distinguish only actual interaction sites—portal entry cells, phased bridge cells, and rotator cells—while collapsing ordinary positions in the same island.\n- The tightened planner is progressing through depth 2 with a stable queue rather than exploding by cell. It has covered the remote controller and hub components so far; no exit signature or reward yet. I’m letting it advance through the first physical-control layer, where an unmodeled route should become visible.\n- The first planner pass was spending most states on the already exhausted 19-position ring graph. I stopped it at 115 canonical states and removed ring movement from this pass. This isolates the avatar/bridge/teleport system and should reach meaningful control depth much faster; ring movement can be reintroduced only if that subsystem is proven insufficient.\n- The avatar-only pass is stable at roughly 150 queued components and is finishing depth 2; most cost now comes from the 29-cell hub/bridge closure, not duplicated ring states. No reward or exit tile has appeared yet.\n- The search is at 205 canonical states and still closing depth 3. The shortest verified return from the controller into the physical rotator requires four control transitions, so the next layer is the first one that can expose a genuinely new route; I’m continuing to that boundary.\n- A more important context was not yet tested: the four D-pad buttons may be enabled by matching colored floor targets outside the remote selector. The central physical target uses color 12, the same code as the selector’s left branch. I’m staging the avatar on that target in both rotator orientations and probing all direction controls; this could allow the avatar and ring to interact cooperatively from separate regions.\n- The controller-origin search reached depth 4 without exposing a new route before the harness’s execution window closed. I’m restarting the same normalized search from the verified physical transfer checkpoint. This removes the four-control bridge prefix and spends the search budget on TOP/MAIN/selector interactions around the full 68-cell physical component.\n- The physical-root pass confirmed its own duplication source: it was treating every rotator cell as a distinct control site. I’ve narrowed controls to the verified pivot, portal entry cells, and exact phased-bridge cells. Those are the only places where MAIN or TOP has position-dependent behavior in our reproduced observations.\n- The narrowed physical search is now compact: 35 canonical states, depth 2, with both the 68-cell rotator component and the 26-cell phased-bridge component represented. No exit or reward has appeared yet.\n\n## Preserved local probes\n\n- `capture_l6_frames.py` (1325 bytes)\n- `capture_l6_ring_montage.py` (2345 bytes)\n- `capture_level5_initial.py` (871 bytes)\n- `probe_goal_semantics.py` (2094 bytes)\n- `probe_l6.py` (1875 bytes)\n- `probe_l6_aboard_bfs.py` (2157 bytes)\n- `probe_l6_all_docks.py` (2144 bytes)\n- `probe_l6_bfs.py` (2059 bytes)\n- `probe_l6_bridge_avatar.py` (3965 bytes)\n- `probe_l6_candidate.py` (3614 bytes)\n- `probe_l6_canonical_macro_bfs.py` (4434 bytes)\n- `probe_l6_cardinal_exits.py` (1019 bytes)\n- `probe_l6_cargo_all_positions.py` (1045 bytes)\n- `probe_l6_cargo_bfs.py` (2728 bytes)\n- `probe_l6_cargo_roundtrip.py` (1881 bytes)\n- `probe_l6_cargo_top.py` (2621 bytes)\n- `probe_l6_cargo_top_combo.py` (1591 bytes)\n- `probe_l6_cargo_top_horizontal.py` (1127 bytes)\n- `probe_l6_cargo_top_middle.py` (1453 bytes)\n- `probe_l6_context.py` (3157 bytes)\n- `probe_l6_cumulative.py` (1504 bytes)\n- `probe_l6_cycles.py` (1087 bytes)\n- `probe_l6_destinations.py` (2164 bytes)\n- `probe_l6_dock_dense_click.py` (1266 bytes)\n- `probe_l6_dock_panel_scan.py` (1688 bytes)\n- `probe_l6_dock_then_top.py` (1533 bytes)\n- `probe_l6_dpad.py` (1840 